# Keep the build context small and hermetic. The image builds BOTH halves of the product: the `web`
# stage needs web/ (sources, manifest and lockfile) plus scripts/build-web.sh, and the build stage
# needs the Go module graph and the Go sources. Nothing else belongs in the context, and a fat
# context slows every `docker build` and every layer-cache miss.
#
# This is an allowlist-by-exclusion: everything below is either already-built output (which the
# multi-stage build regenerates), tooling that never reaches the binary, or local cruft that would
# invalidate the cache on every commit.

# Version control and CI.
.git
.github
.gitignore
.dockerignore

# Agent and editor local state — never part of a build.
.claude
.vscode
.idea

# Build output. The image builds both the SPA and the binary itself; a stale ./bin or a laptop's
# web/dist must not leak in. The `web` stage regenerates web/dist and stages it over
# internal/ui/dist, so what the context carries there is only ever the committed placeholder — which
# the build stage deletes before copying the real output in.
/bin
/dist
web/dist
web/node_modules
node_modules
coverage.out
# The per-checkout golangci-lint cache (issue #117). Hundreds of megabytes of analysis results that
# no build stage reads, and every `make lint` invalidates the layer that would carry them.
/.cache

# Docs and design. Served from embed.FS by the binary when they are embedded; the source tree here
# is not needed to compile, and the mockups are large.
docs/design/mockups

# The container definition and deploy assets do not go into their own build context.
deploy
Dockerfile

# Test fixtures and golden data are large and never linked into the binary.
test/fixtures
test/golden

# OS junk.
.DS_Store
*.swp
